Subject: [PATCH] Manage the runtime resources Nonoka X installs

Nonoka X has no resource system of its own: every managed binary it can find
lives under `finesub/runtime/<id>/`, resolved by `managedtools`. So what the
desktop can install is decided here, in the engine's resource manifest, and this
patch holds every difference from upstream's list. It has three parts, which
can be removed separately.

Video downloader tools. aria2c, node and pot-provider are declared because a
tool has to be in the manifest to be installable at all. All three are optional:
without them the downloader still runs, it is just slower and fails on anything
but short videos.

  aria2c         Multi-connection fetch. Pure speed; the built-in downloader
                 works without it.
  node           A JavaScript runtime. yt-dlp needs one to solve the "n"
                 challenge (it enables only deno by default), and the PO token
                 generator runs on it.
  pot-provider   Proof-of-origin tokens. YouTube only returns usable formats to
                 a signed-in session whose request carries one, and yt-dlp
                 cannot mint them itself.

They are declared together because they only work together: YouTube gates in
sequence, and a missing piece fails at an earlier gate than the one it belongs
to, which is what makes each look useless when added alone.

`pot-provider` carries both halves of the provider -- the yt-dlp plugin and the
generator it calls. Upstream ships no built backend (it needs an npm install
with a native `canvas`, which is not a single archive), and versions the two
halves together, so Nonoka X builds and publishes one bundle instead: see
`scripts/build_pot_bundle.py`. It is GPL-3.0, and the bundle carries the licence
and corresponding source accordingly. aria2c's binary and node's sit one
directory down inside their archives, so `required_files` names those paths.

No managed Git. FineSub's knowledge store is SQLite and no runtime path uses
Git anymore. Keeping MinGit in the manifest still exposes a download/install
target, adds it to diagnostics, and injects its directory into every worker
PATH. The managed asset and the now-dead system-tool plumbing are removed.
Repository maintenance remains a developer concern outside the packaged runtime.

A pinned FFmpeg. The manifest followed BtbN's `latest` tag, whose bytes change
on every build, and asked the GitHub release API for the digest at install time.
A copy kept on Nonoka's own storage as the last download rung cannot be verified
against a digest that moves daily, so the asset is pinned to a month-end
autobuild, the kind the builder retains.

Removal: delete the tools once upstream declares them, the Git part once
upstream removes managed Git, and the pin once FFmpeg is no longer self-hosted.

